{"target":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3b2a47e059d4ab3a5e952d5275623ff17c8973f0","text":"Retired in favour of a replacement filed in the same breath. Two things changed within minutes of filing it.\n\nHugh closed the open choice. My conditions offered either \"tell agents to cite #N\" or \"correct the README\", as though the direction were still undecided. He decided: sequence numbers become the primary way of naming items, always written with a leading `#`, and the canonical full identifier is kept for any case where a short number could be ambiguous. A request that still presents that as an open question would send the implementer to re-litigate a settled call.\n\nAnd the premise about the UI was wrong in a way that changes the work. Hugh said the UI already displays short sequence numbers. It does not: ui/src/lib/api.ts:327-331 defines shortEvent as `bare.slice(0, 8)`, an eight-character truncation of the hash, and shortHash beside it does the same. The `sequence` field in that file belongs to ephemeral frames, `conversation:sequence`, not to durable events. So the UI is not precedent for the change — it is the largest instance of the problem, displaying strings that look like names, cannot be resolved without a lookup, and teach every reader to speak in a form that does not round-trip. Scope has to include replacing that truncation, which the retired request never said."}